  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+\u2026+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]\u0394x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,6],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=6.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{6-0}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(2)+f(4))+f(6)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $38$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [2+2(6+18)+38]\\cdot 2=88\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[0,6],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore the approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
